--- body ---
## Context

`settings.shared.json` is the tracked canonical configuration. Claude Code also
writes machine-owned values to the ignored live `settings.json`;
`settings.local.json` carries machine-specific additions. The 0976 cleanup
deleted several hooks and their scripts from the shared file, but the live file
still called `knowledge_hints.py`. `UserPromptSubmit` then blocked a prompt.
On 2026-09-24 the live hooks were manually copied from the shared file,
which repairs this instance but leaves the drift mechanism manual.

The former `scripts/check-settings-drift.sh` was deleted by 0976 because its
output was discarded at session start. Reintroducing a report-only warning
would repeat that failure. This ticket now owns an explicit, testable alignment
command, including a way to distinguish aligned from unable-to-inspect.

## Relevant files

- `settings.shared.json` — tracked canonical settings
- `settings.json` — ignored live settings, written in part by Claude Code
- `settings.local.json` — ignored machine-specific settings
- `scripts/on-start.sh` — existing session entry point
- `Makefile` — operator command

## Actions

1. Declare which top-level keys belong to the CLI, at least `model`,
   `effortLevel`, `feedbackDrafts`, `agentPushNotifEnabled`, and
   `modelSettings`. Preserve their live values during alignment.
2. Build `scripts/settings-align.sh` with `--check` and apply modes. Merge
   canonical repo-owned values with preserved CLI-owned values; route
   machine-specific additions through `settings.local.json`. Write atomically
   and make repeated application byte-identical.
3. Make `--check` report individual missing, added, and changed hook commands
   and permission rules. Return a distinct error when either file cannot be
   read or parsed. Do not silently turn that error into `aligned`.
4. Add `make settings-align`. Decide whether `on-start.sh` should surface a
   concise warning only after the check demonstrates useful, visible output;
   the operator command is required regardless of startup wiring.
5. Verify the live file once with the new command, without changing
   CLI-owned values or broadening permissions.

## Verification

- [ ] A fixture with a deleted hook names that hook before alignment and is clean after
- [ ] A fixture with a removed permission rule names the rule before alignment
- [ ] CLI-owned values survive; machine-specific settings remain in their local layer
- [ ] Two alignments produce identical bytes
- [ ] Unreadable or malformed input is visibly different from aligned
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- `settings.json` and `settings.local.json` remain ignored.
- Alignment carries canonical policy as written; it never widens a rule.
- A startup check, if enabled, must not block a session on inspection failure.

## Exit criteria

The live file can be rebuilt with one command, and its check gives actionable
per-rule drift or an explicit inspection failure.
